00:05:30.231 --> 00:05:34.211
So with this … So you can very
simply ask AI to supply this image

00:05:34.211 --> 00:05:36.591
and say, "I want to extract out
my characters," because I want

00:05:36.601 --> 00:05:37.981
to use them as reference images.

00:05:38.231 --> 00:05:41.041
And for this one I did it within Codex,
so you can see it's managed to extract the

00:05:41.041 --> 00:05:42.581
character out of the background over here.

00:05:42.921 --> 00:05:44.581
and you can see this is the
green boxer that I managed to

00:05:44.581 --> 00:05:48.581
extract out along with the jujitsu
fighter that you see over here.

00:05:48.921 --> 00:05:51.871
So that's very simple because you
can make use of image generation as

00:05:51.871 --> 00:05:55.471
well as these mock-ups to pull all
of these out as reference images.

00:05:55.811 --> 00:05:58.321
And the reason why you want to get
all of these reference images is

00:05:58.321 --> 00:06:02.261
that these reference images are
going to form like the basis of

00:06:02.321 --> 00:06:03.651
all your subsequent animations.

00:06:04.951 --> 00:06:05.891
All right, so now we have that.

00:06:05.911 --> 00:06:08.831
Now we go about to animating the
characters so that we are gonna

00:06:08.831 --> 00:06:10.151
be able to put them into the game.

00:06:10.401 --> 00:06:13.571
And for this, I made use of a tool that
I've been building called Spriterific,

00:06:13.931 --> 00:06:17.341
And the goal of this tool that you
see over here is that given a supplied

00:06:17.341 --> 00:06:22.301
source image, it will process it
to create a game-ready sprite that

00:06:22.311 --> 00:06:24.241
you can then use within your game.

00:06:24.501 --> 00:06:27.841
and I've got several videos on how
you can do this step-by-step using

00:06:27.861 --> 00:06:32.041
any image generation model, whether
it's NanoBanana 2 or Images 2.0.

00:06:32.291 --> 00:06:35.271
but the whole purpose of building this
tool is so that I can have a single

00:06:35.271 --> 00:06:38.841
place that allows me to do this very
easily and to visualize the results.

00:06:38.951 --> 00:06:42.491
So let me just show you the prompt
that I use as part of Spriteriffic.

00:06:42.561 --> 00:06:45.321
so for example, I said, "Given the
character reference image, think

00:06:45.321 --> 00:06:48.021
about the images that we're gonna
use." And then all I needed to do

00:06:48.021 --> 00:06:51.471
is say, "Let's use Spriteriffic
to generate the anchor image."

00:06:51.761 --> 00:06:55.351
This will go ahead and automatically
create what we call an anchor

00:06:55.391 --> 00:06:56.571
image that you have over here.

00:06:56.651 --> 00:07:00.271
So given a source image, it will
normalize everything, puts it against

00:07:00.271 --> 00:07:05.581
a green screen and creates a neutral
facing anchor that will serve as

00:07:05.581 --> 00:07:09.001
the basis for image consistency
for the rest of our animations.

00:07:09.017 --> 00:07:12.217
and all I have to say is say, "Let's use
Spriterrific on the chosen anchor for the

00:07:12.217 --> 00:07:16.237
two attack animations." And what it will
do is that it will go ahead and generate

00:07:16.247 --> 00:07:17.347
the animations that you see over here.

00:07:18.087 --> 00:07:22.337
and how it does so is that, first of
all, it uses Groq Imagine to create

00:07:22.377 --> 00:07:24.387
a movie version of the animation.

00:07:24.417 --> 00:07:26.827
So you can see that this is
the movie version over here.

00:07:27.087 --> 00:07:30.777
The next thing it does is that it will
take out every single frame of this video

00:07:30.777 --> 00:07:32.307
that you see over here and lay it out,

00:07:32.537 --> 00:07:34.957
And then after that, it will go
ahead and pick a subset of these

00:07:34.957 --> 00:07:38.727
frames and stitch it together into a
sprite sheet that you see over here.

00:07:39.077 --> 00:07:42.407
And then that's how you get your
animation that you use within the game.

00:07:42.807 --> 00:07:46.237
And Spriterrific is quite useful because
So you can actually pick the frames that

00:07:46.237 --> 00:07:47.787
you want rather than using the default.

00:07:48.027 --> 00:07:51.617
And then you can do some additional
things, such as aligning your

00:07:51.617 --> 00:07:53.067
sprite if there's any drift.

00:07:53.347 --> 00:07:56.527
I find that when you use Groq Imagine,
you don't actually have much drift

00:07:56.527 --> 00:08:00.407
because it's all generated from
a video, and this already ensures

00:08:00.407 --> 00:08:01.697
that everything looks consistent.

00:08:01.797 --> 00:08:05.157
But it's always useful to be able to
have that level of control to be able

00:08:05.157 --> 00:08:06.737
to adjust your images accordingly.

00:08:08.057 --> 00:08:10.407
so you can see several
runs that I did here.

00:08:10.407 --> 00:08:11.617
this is the heavy kick run.

00:08:11.667 --> 00:08:15.877
this is the kick animation that was
generated from this, Groq Imagine video.

00:08:16.207 --> 00:08:19.497
And then once I knew that it was
working correctly, I then went ahead

00:08:19.557 --> 00:08:21.807
and said, "Go ahead and generate
all the different animations that

00:08:21.807 --> 00:08:25.237
we would need for this game." So you
can see we have a blocking animation.

00:08:25.557 --> 00:08:26.477
We have crouch.

00:08:26.727 --> 00:08:27.597
we have being hit.

00:08:27.747 --> 00:08:30.387
We have an idle animation,
and we have a jump, and then

00:08:30.387 --> 00:08:31.727
we have a knockdown as well.

00:08:31.927 --> 00:08:37.037
And you can see here, sometimes if your
prompt, isn't correct, can result in,

00:08:37.087 --> 00:08:38.447
animations that don't really look right.

00:08:38.447 --> 00:08:40.927
So in this case, he shouldn't really
be falling with his hit on the left.

00:08:41.267 --> 00:08:44.697
I just went ahead and just re-prompted
it so that I was able to get the,

00:08:44.837 --> 00:08:45.897
right animation that I wanted.

00:08:45.974 --> 00:08:49.524
And you can see here I did the same
thing, with this, jujitsu fighter,

00:08:49.584 --> 00:08:52.274
and I did do it for the boxer as
well, but I just decided not to

00:08:52.274 --> 00:08:53.074
use this character in the game.

00:08:53.074 --> 00:08:55.194
But you can see SpriteRefit
makes it really easy.

00:08:55.244 --> 00:08:57.844
You can pass in the source image
and then prompt whatever animations

00:08:57.844 --> 00:09:01.914
that you need, and it automatically,
gives you game-ready sprite sheets

00:09:01.954 --> 00:09:03.504
that you can put into the game

00:09:03.604 --> 00:09:06.554
I've not released SpriteRefit yet.

00:09:06.554 --> 00:09:06.721
I'm still working on it.

00:09:06.721 --> 00:09:08.414
But if you're interested, I put
a link in the description below

00:09:08.414 --> 00:09:10.894
where you can register your
interest, for development updates.

00:09:10.947 --> 00:09:13.557
So now that we've got all of
our animations, the thing that

00:09:13.567 --> 00:09:16.827
I always like to do is to create
something called a character gem.

00:09:17.267 --> 00:09:19.977
If you watch some of my previous
videos, you will know why I do this.

00:09:20.177 --> 00:09:22.657
but I'm gonna go through
the key elements of a gem.

00:09:23.137 --> 00:09:27.097
now the whole purpose of a, of a gem
is to allow you to visualize and to

00:09:27.097 --> 00:09:30.341
debug and to troubleshoot the animation
sprite sheets that you have over

00:09:30.341 --> 00:09:32.661
here, but in a nice single place.

00:09:32.701 --> 00:09:35.471
So you can see over here, I can
choose whichever character I

00:09:35.471 --> 00:09:39.021
want, and look through any of the
animations that I want as well.

00:09:39.371 --> 00:09:43.591
the key elements are I need to see the
number of frames in the animations,

00:09:43.621 --> 00:09:46.431
and I should be able to slow it
down, and pause whenever I want.

00:09:46.771 --> 00:09:49.881
But the most important thing is that
we have these thing called bounds.

00:09:50.161 --> 00:09:53.441
all of the logic is going to
be based on not on the entire

00:09:53.461 --> 00:09:55.911
image, but on bounds or hitboxes.

00:09:56.271 --> 00:09:59.101
in this case, you want separate
hitboxes for collision.

00:09:59.151 --> 00:10:02.741
for example, this one will be how
the character lands on the ground.

00:10:02.841 --> 00:10:05.581
and the main reason why this is very
important is that sometimes you will

00:10:05.581 --> 00:10:08.281
have additional padding below your
character, and You want to make sure

00:10:08.281 --> 00:10:11.928
that the collision of the character
with the ground looks correct so the

00:10:11.928 --> 00:10:15.588
main bounds that you will need to have
are your collision bounds, and then

00:10:15.778 --> 00:10:17.318
you want to have your, hit bounds.

00:10:17.328 --> 00:10:20.778
So this will mean that as long
as there's an overlap within

00:10:20.778 --> 00:10:22.588
this area, a hit will register.

00:10:22.928 --> 00:10:24.688
But then this is the key one.

00:10:25.508 --> 00:10:27.078
but this is the key one
that we have over here.

00:10:27.128 --> 00:10:30.368
If we take a look at this punch
animation and this kick, you will

00:10:30.368 --> 00:10:34.558
notice that the attack bounds only
becomes active at certain frames.

00:10:34.968 --> 00:10:39.018
So at the fifth, sixth, and seventh
frame, this is where the attack bounds

00:10:39.108 --> 00:10:44.188
actually shows up, because you don't
want the attack to register before

00:10:44.188 --> 00:10:47.328
the character has even finished
animating his leg sticking out, okay?

00:10:47.558 --> 00:10:50.408
And you don't want it to register
when he's withdrawing his leg as well.

00:10:50.568 --> 00:10:52.568
this applies to all of
the different characters.

00:10:52.568 --> 00:10:56.868
So you can see here, if I do, a heavy
kick, it only registers the attack

00:10:56.888 --> 00:10:59.188
bounds when it is up over here.

00:10:59.208 --> 00:11:00.978
And this allows me to adjust it slightly.

00:11:01.368 --> 00:11:02.508
and then I can just save this.

00:11:02.708 --> 00:11:07.098
And this editor allows you to ensure that
your characters have the right kind of

00:11:07.098 --> 00:11:11.614
animations, and the hitboxes that you have
are activated only at the correct frames.

00:11:11.804 --> 00:11:15.054
because you're working with spreadsheets
over here, not every single frame is

00:11:15.094 --> 00:11:18.714
going to be relevant when it comes to
particular actions one of the main things

00:11:18.714 --> 00:11:22.424
I learned about building a fighting
game is that if you have a blocking

00:11:22.424 --> 00:11:24.594
mechanic, which reduces the damage,

00:11:24.814 --> 00:11:29.064
You will want to have an additional type
of bounds called guard, because this

00:11:29.084 --> 00:11:33.044
guard box is important because if you have
different kind of attacks, so for example,

00:11:33.044 --> 00:11:36.884
if you have a heavy attack that kicks high
and the person ends up guarding low, you

00:11:36.884 --> 00:11:38.584
don't want the guard to register, right?

00:11:38.864 --> 00:11:41.454
vice versa, if you are punching at
the top and the person's guarding

00:11:41.454 --> 00:11:43.434
low, you might want the attack to hit.

00:11:44.004 --> 00:11:46.434
all right, so that's the character
gym, and once you've gotten hold of

00:11:46.434 --> 00:11:49.444
that, that's pretty much the key set
of things that you will really need

00:11:49.574 --> 00:11:51.134
to be able to get your game working.

00:11:51.524 --> 00:11:55.334
So the other thing that I always like to
do is just before I create the main game,

00:11:55.354 --> 00:11:56.734
is to create something like a playground.

00:11:56.894 --> 00:12:00.804
And you can see over here, this allows
me to very quickly ensure that things

00:12:00.884 --> 00:12:03.734
are working correctly based on what
I've configured in the character gym.

00:12:04.014 --> 00:12:07.404
So I'm gonna turn on all the bounds that
you see over here, and you can see if

00:12:07.404 --> 00:12:12.154
I try to do an attack here, the attack
bounce only comes out when the hand has

00:12:12.374 --> 00:12:14.404
emerged, and the same thing for the kick.

00:12:14.674 --> 00:12:14.984
Okay?

00:12:15.274 --> 00:12:17.694
and the other thing that you wanna
do over here is that you want to

00:12:17.694 --> 00:12:22.184
take this opportunity to set up
configurable parameters, so you can

00:12:22.184 --> 00:12:24.004
just tweak the feel of the character.

00:12:24.024 --> 00:12:26.594
sometimes you will see that the
scale of your character may be a

00:12:26.594 --> 00:12:29.824
little bit off because of the way
that the sprites get normalized.

00:12:29.934 --> 00:12:32.474
this allows you to very quickly
just adjust because I want this

00:12:32.474 --> 00:12:35.954
character to be a lot larger than
what, the default scale looks like.

00:12:36.204 --> 00:12:38.114
The other thing that you can do
is you can adjust the speed here.

00:12:38.164 --> 00:12:40.484
I change it to a thousand, you
can see that I move a lot quicker.

00:12:40.794 --> 00:12:43.744
And then if I change it to one, you can
see this character moves very slowly.

00:12:43.744 --> 00:12:46.154
So this is very important
as part of game development.

00:12:46.164 --> 00:12:49.244
AI is not gonna be able to make your
game feel perfectly right, so you want

00:12:49.244 --> 00:12:52.134
to ensure that you have the opportunity
to tweak these parameters as well,

00:12:52.134 --> 00:12:55.934
because otherwise, you will find
that it just feels a little bit off.

00:12:56.254 --> 00:12:56.564
Okay?

00:12:56.774 --> 00:12:59.184
And the main thing, that you want to
do over here, whenever you're working

00:12:59.184 --> 00:13:04.144
with any kind of playground game, is
I always say that I want to be able

00:13:04.144 --> 00:13:08.174
to save all of these parameters into
a JSON file, as you can see over here.

00:13:08.664 --> 00:13:13.404
And then this JSON file is what is
loaded when the actual game starts.

00:13:13.974 --> 00:13:14.264
All right?

00:13:14.414 --> 00:13:17.904
So you can see all of these values
that you see over here are loaded

00:13:17.914 --> 00:13:19.584
in from that same JSON file.

00:13:19.584 --> 00:13:22.224
So just say you're playing the main
game now, and you've spot something

00:13:22.224 --> 00:13:25.714
that is wrong, you can at least tweak
it straight away, and then you can save

00:13:25.714 --> 00:13:29.454
it, and you have a consistent place to
ensure that your experience is correct.

00:13:29.655 --> 00:13:32.735
one of the things that I realized is
that when I generated reverse walking

00:13:32.735 --> 00:13:34.995
animations, it just didn't look right.

00:13:34.995 --> 00:13:38.875
So I just changed the logic over
here, and I said play the walking

00:13:38.875 --> 00:13:41.965
animation in reverse, and this
gave me a much better result.

00:13:42.145 --> 00:13:45.035
So this is the reason why you want to
have a playground, because it allows you

00:13:45.045 --> 00:13:48.185
to tweak and play around with how your
characters are gonna look like and how

00:13:48.185 --> 00:13:51.725
the gameplay is gonna feel like before you
start wiring it in all within the game.

00:13:51.725 --> 00:13:53.295
So that's the playground
that you have over there.

00:13:53.625 --> 00:13:57.805
the next thing that I did was I just went
into Codex and said I wanted to generate

00:13:57.805 --> 00:13:59.005
some portraits of these characters.

00:13:59.095 --> 00:14:01.965
So you can see here, let's generate
stylized portraits of the characters

00:14:01.965 --> 00:14:03.165
based on the reference images.

00:14:03.555 --> 00:14:07.635
I want it to have their upper body, in
distinctive poses, and I want to use

00:14:07.635 --> 00:14:09.425
them in a character selection screen.

00:14:09.775 --> 00:14:12.055
and then you can see here I'm
using the Image Gen Skill.

00:14:12.055 --> 00:14:15.535
And you can see it generated these ones
that we have over here, and this was

00:14:15.535 --> 00:14:17.265
fairly simple to integrate into game.

00:14:17.515 --> 00:14:21.075
so you can see I made use of these
within the character selection screen.

00:14:21.525 --> 00:14:23.515
And this was the time where
I said I wanted to be able to

00:14:23.515 --> 00:14:24.785
select between different levels.

00:14:25.055 --> 00:14:26.185
I also wanted a 1v1 versus a one-

00:14:29.475 --> 00:14:29.915
1vAI, setting.

00:14:30.235 --> 00:14:34.255
this allows me to play against AI if you
don't have a second player to play with.

00:14:34.805 --> 00:14:38.235
And I deliberately asked the AI to
be fairly naive here, but you can ask

00:14:38.235 --> 00:14:41.455
your agent to make the AI a lot more
realistic, and you have a solid result

00:14:41.791 --> 00:14:45.431
All right, so once you've got the main
menu set up, the main thing is to go

00:14:45.431 --> 00:14:47.171
into the core combat loop over here.

00:14:47.481 --> 00:14:50.241
this one is really just about
you going ahead and thinking

00:14:50.241 --> 00:14:51.591
about what goes into the game.

00:14:51.791 --> 00:14:53.331
So we add a countdown timer.

00:14:53.541 --> 00:14:57.421
we also say that we want it such that
we have health for the characters, and

00:14:57.421 --> 00:15:00.391
when the health depletes, as you see
over here, it starts a second round,

00:15:00.541 --> 00:15:04.231
replenishes the health, and you can see in
this little icon that we have over here,

00:15:04.521 --> 00:15:05.701
it means that I've won the first round.

00:15:05.701 --> 00:15:07.631
So this is very typical
of a fighting game.

00:15:07.761 --> 00:15:10.591
And I just said that, you know,
it's best of three matches, before

00:15:10.591 --> 00:15:11.861
the game goes into game over.

00:15:12.811 --> 00:15:14.541
So that's the core combat loop.

00:15:14.801 --> 00:15:17.161
next thing to talk about
are the UI elements.

00:15:17.204 --> 00:15:19.764
I just said, "Let's
generate a UI atlas," right?

00:15:19.764 --> 00:15:21.514
Using image generation that
we can use for the game.

00:15:21.704 --> 00:15:24.174
We need a health bar with appropriate
transparent areas for the health

00:15:24.174 --> 00:15:26.774
fill and a portrait base to put
the character's portrait in.

00:15:27.024 --> 00:15:28.644
And this is what it generated.

00:15:28.674 --> 00:15:31.804
You can see, it's already, put
against a chroma background, so you

00:15:31.804 --> 00:15:34.634
can then use the image generation
skill to make this transparent.

00:15:34.834 --> 00:15:37.124
And you can see that these are
all the different elements that

00:15:37.124 --> 00:15:38.284
it uses as part of the game.

00:15:38.644 --> 00:15:41.284
These are the props that I
generated because I intended to

00:15:41.284 --> 00:15:43.814
put, props in the background, but
I never got around to doing it.

00:15:43.994 --> 00:15:47.764
And then after that, you can
simply prompt your agent to

00:15:47.774 --> 00:15:48.924
integrate this within your game.

00:15:48.924 --> 00:15:49.604
this was one prompt.

00:15:49.614 --> 00:15:51.334
I said, "Let's integrate
UI elements in the game.

00:15:51.334 --> 00:15:53.094
We should lay out the health
of the two characters and their

00:15:53.094 --> 00:15:54.254
portraits, Make it look good.

00:15:54.514 --> 00:15:57.434
The health bar should be dynamic and
change color based on the amount of

00:15:57.434 --> 00:16:00.174
health left, and start blinking." this
was the effect that you see over here,

00:16:00.484 --> 00:16:04.284
Once, the health starts depleting,
it goes from green, and then goes to

00:16:04.284 --> 00:16:08.814
yellow, and then finally when it's
red it starts blinking, and then when

00:16:08.814 --> 00:16:11.744
the health depletes, the character
loses and plays the death animation.

00:16:12.224 --> 00:16:13.584
Okay, so it's really good.

00:16:13.634 --> 00:16:16.734
I find that it does
really well in using AI.

00:16:16.734 --> 00:16:19.934
I find that, the models are really
good at integrating UI elements.

00:16:19.954 --> 00:16:22.824
Like you can see here, I just used
a single prompt and it was able to

00:16:22.824 --> 00:16:24.354
get everything working correctly.

00:16:24.613 --> 00:16:26.293
all right, so that's pretty much it.

00:16:26.303 --> 00:16:29.533
Once you've got all of these things, you
have a complete fighting game over here.

00:16:29.713 --> 00:16:32.083
and you can see this is a really
very decent result, and it's really

00:16:32.083 --> 00:16:34.643
pretty fun to play, and you can
play this on the same machine.

00:16:34.913 --> 00:16:37.763
and if you want to make this a fully
multiplayer game, you can probably use

00:16:37.773 --> 00:16:40.753
something like Colyseus, and I've got a
