00:16:40.753 --> 00:16:44.323
vibe code multiplayer functionality, very
quite easily using the Colyseus library.

00:16:44.803 --> 00:16:47.373
the last thing that I wanted
to cover is that I wanted to

00:16:47.373 --> 00:16:48.683
have something called specials.

00:16:48.773 --> 00:16:52.233
and so this was the final thing that I
just wanted to have just as a nice way

00:16:52.233 --> 00:16:54.153
to polish up what we have over here.

00:16:54.453 --> 00:16:56.153
And in this case it was very simple.

00:16:56.733 --> 00:16:59.323
I said I wanted to have
a special move system.

00:16:59.323 --> 00:17:01.943
This will involve hitting a specials
button when the meter is filled.

00:17:02.443 --> 00:17:05.463
And I did say that, I wanted to have
different specials for different

00:17:05.463 --> 00:17:10.743
characters, and then, I said you need a
charging special animation, and then, it

00:17:10.743 --> 00:17:14.656
should, register multiple hits as opposed
to just being a single hit, if you're

00:17:14.656 --> 00:17:17.926
doing a special attack, So let me just
explain this in a little bit more detail.

00:17:18.086 --> 00:17:21.846
So if I play as this character,
and I'm gonna toggle the special.

00:17:22.276 --> 00:17:26.986
You can see over here, he's gonna do
something like a spinaroonie, and then you

00:17:26.986 --> 00:17:32.336
can see that it registers multiple hits,
in a combo when he's able to perform his

00:17:32.336 --> 00:17:33.776
special, and that's what I wanted to do.

00:17:34.216 --> 00:17:37.956
Once I prompted the mechanics, it
implemented it, and then just made

00:17:37.956 --> 00:17:40.916
use of Spriteriffic once again
to, generate all of these special

00:17:40.916 --> 00:17:42.466
animations that you can see over here.

00:17:42.776 --> 00:17:45.736
and then it automatically picked the
best frames stitched them together, and

00:17:45.736 --> 00:17:47.336
then exported it out as a spreadsheet.

00:17:47.336 --> 00:17:48.546
so this was very easy to do.

00:17:48.756 --> 00:17:52.556
I then just tweaked it a little bit saying
that I wanted, when the specials, happens,

00:17:52.586 --> 00:17:56.166
I wanted to show the portrait, and then
I wanted to have this lightbox effect

00:17:56.176 --> 00:17:59.286
where the portrait comes in from the left
to give it a bit of a specials effect.

00:18:00.136 --> 00:18:00.896
so there you have it.

00:18:00.896 --> 00:18:04.356
That's how you can go ahead and build
your very own Street Fighter-like game

00:18:04.476 --> 00:18:08.516
using AI, and you can just follow the
workflow that I use from start to finish,

00:18:08.616 --> 00:18:09.876
You just wanna take things step by step.

00:18:09.876 --> 00:18:12.656
Don't try to single problem and say, "Ah,
build me a Street Fighter clone." You'll

00:18:12.656 --> 00:18:16.536
probably get a fairly okay result, but
whenever you're working on games, you

00:18:16.536 --> 00:18:19.876
want to take things slowly, build things
iteratively, make sure that things look

00:18:19.876 --> 00:18:21.946
right before you move on to the next step.

00:18:22.176 --> 00:18:25.826
whether you're building a fighting game,
a platformer, an RPG, or whatever, is to

00:18:25.866 --> 00:18:30.016
always have gyms, so that you can test
things like the character animations.

00:18:30.226 --> 00:18:32.446
You can test things like the
background, And then always have a

00:18:32.446 --> 00:18:36.006
playground, so that you're gonna be
able to test and tweak things like

00:18:36.016 --> 00:18:37.736
the values of the mechanics as well.

